findings.body:
**Security:** password-reset tokens are no longer cryptographically secure and are far too short.

`Math.random()` is not a CSPRNG. Multiplying by `1e12` yields at most ~10^12 possible values (~40 bits of entropy) versus the previous `randomBytes(32)` (256 bits, 64 hex chars). An attacker who can request resets or try tokens against the redeem endpoint can brute-force this space; PRNG predictability makes offline or targeted guessing worse.

Node `crypto.randomBytes` is unavailable on the edge, but **Web Crypto is**. Use `crypto.getRandomValues` so the token stays 32 secure bytes:

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```

Do not ship reset tokens derived from `Math.random()`.
